'use strict';

const lessons = {};
function lesson(id, group, title, slides, summary, flow, example, questions) {
 lessons[id] = {group,title,slides,summary,flow,example,questions};
}
lesson('sender','IPv4 journey','From application data to a frame','Switching 31–33; Delivery 3–10',
 'The sender chooses the next-hop IP before building the Ethernet frame. The IPv4 destination names the final host. The Ethernet destination names the device that receives this link’s frame.',
 ['Transport hands IP a segment','IP adds source, destination and protocol','Compare destination with local prefix','Resolve local target or gateway','Build and transmit Ethernet frame'],
 'Host A is 192.0.2.10/24. A packet for 203.0.113.20 goes to the gateway at 192.0.2.1. The frame carries the gateway MAC.',
 [['Which IP does ARP ask for?','The gateway’s local IP in indirect delivery. For a directly connected destination it asks for the destination host’s IP.'],['Why does IP survive decapsulation?','Ethernet removes its own header and trailer and passes the enclosed IP datagram upward.']]);
lesson('ingress','IPv4 journey','Incoming interface','Switching 33–35',
 'A router receives signals and reconstructs the Ethernet frame. Its data-link layer checks frame integrity and hands the IPv4 datagram to the network layer.',
 ['Receive bits','Check Ethernet frame and FCS','Read EtherType','Strip Ethernet framing','Hand IPv4 datagram to IP'],
 'An IPv4 Ethernet frame uses EtherType 0x0800. An ARP frame uses 0x0806.',
 [['Why check both FCS and IP checksum?','FCS protects the current Ethernet frame on one link. The IPv4 checksum covers just the IPv4 header.'],['Does the exact incoming frame get forwarded?','The outgoing interface constructs a new link-layer frame for the next link.']]);
lesson('validate','IPv4 journey','IPv4 validation and dispatch','Switching 34–35',
 'The router checks whether it has a well-formed IPv4 datagram. A valid packet can be for the router itself or can enter the forwarding path.',
 ['Check version, header length and total length','Verify the IPv4 header checksum','Malformed → discard','Local destination → deliver upward','Transit destination → lookup'],
 'A corrupted IPv4 header is discarded. A valid OSPF packet addressed to a local OSPF endpoint can be handed to the routing process using IP protocol 89.',
 [['Will a corrupted packet always generate ICMP?','No. For example a bad IPv4 header checksum is silently discarded.'],['What does the Protocol field do?','It identifies the IP payload, for example TCP, UDP, ICMP or OSPF.']]);
lesson('forward','Forwarding','Routing table lookup','Delivery 11–21, 43–56, 65–66',
 'The destination IP is compared with route prefixes. The winning entry tells the forwarding plane the outgoing interface and next-hop IP. A directly connected route uses the destination itself as the next hop.',
 ['Read destination IP','Apply an entry’s prefix mask','Keep entries whose network matches','Select longest matching prefix','Resolve next hop on selected interface'],
 'For 203.0.113.140, both 203.0.113.128/26 and /25 match. /26 wins. A /32 host route can override both and /0 is the fallback.',
 [['How is routing different from forwarding?','Routing learns or configures routes. Forwarding applies the installed entries to a particular packet.'],['What if there is no match or default?','The router cannot forward the datagram. It drops it and may return an ICMP Destination Unreachable.'],['How can a router look up prefixes efficiently?','A prefix trie or tables organized by prefix length can avoid scanning every route.']]);
lesson('classful','Forwarding','Classful and subnetted forwarding','Delivery 22–42, 65',
 'In the historical classful method the leading bits imply the default mask. A router within a fixed-length subnetted site uses the site’s longer mask.',
 ['Identify address class','Choose class mask or known subnet mask','AND destination with mask','Look up the resulting network','Use next hop or direct delivery'],
 '145.14.32.78 AND 255.255.192.0 (/18) is 145.14.0.0. The /18 blocks in the third octet start at 0, 64, 128, and 192.',
 [['What are the old default masks?','Class A /8, Class B /16, Class C /24. Classless routing reads the prefix length from the entry.'],['How do I reconstruct a topology from a route table?','Draw each directly connected subnet on its listed interface. Then draw the listed next-hop routers on those subnets.']]);
lesson('cidr','Forwarding','CIDR aggregation and ISP hierarchy','Delivery 57–66',
 'Adjacent, correctly aligned blocks can be announced as one less-specific prefix. A regional ISP can announce one aggregate while local routers retain detailed customer prefixes.',
 ['Identify contiguous blocks','Check common prefix and alignment','Advertise the aggregate','Retain more-specific exceptions','Forward by longest prefix match'],
 '120.14.64.0/18 contains 16,384 addresses. It divides into /20 blocks beginning at 120.14.64.0, .80.0, .96.0, and .112.0.',
 [['What happens if a customer moves to another ISP?','A valid more-specific announcement can direct that customer’s traffic to the other ISP.'],['What is geographical aggregation?','Grouping related address blocks geographically is one proposed way to reduce table size. Real announcements still depend on topology and policy.']]);
lesson('ttl','IPv4 journey','TTL, checksum and ICMP Time Exceeded','Switching 34; IPv4 forwarding',
 'A forwarded IPv4 packet loses at least one TTL. Since TTL is in the IPv4 header, the router updates the header checksum before transmission.',
 ['Read incoming TTL','Decrement TTL','TTL still positive → update checksum','TTL exhausted → discard','Generate ICMP Time Exceeded when applicable'],
 'TTL 64 becomes 63 at R1 and 62 at R2. TTL 1 at R1 expires there.',
 [['Why do the IP endpoints stay the same?','This example uses ordinary forwarding without NAT. TTL and checksum change while the endpoint addresses stay fixed.'],['Does ICMP reuse the expired packet?','ICMP is a new IP packet addressed back to the source and includes part of the original packet.']]);
lesson('arp','ARP','Resolve the MAC for one hop','ARP deck; Switching 33–34',
 'ARP takes the next-hop IPv4 address selected for the local outgoing link and returns its Ethernet address.',
 ['Check ARP cache','Cache hit → use stored mapping','Cache miss → broadcast who-has','Target sends unicast reply','Cache answer and send waiting frames'],
 'R1 asks for 198.51.100.2 on eth1 and learns R2’s ingress MAC. The datagram can still be addressed to Host B.',
 [['Who receives and who replies?','The LAN receives the request. The target normally replies. Proxy ARP can answer on behalf of another IP.'],['What is in the ARP payload?','Hardware type, protocol type, address lengths, operation, sender MAC/IP, target MAC/IP. Ethernet/IPv4 uses lengths 6 and 4.'],['What is the packet stack?','Ethernet → ARP. The request uses MAC ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff. The reply typically uses the requester’s MAC.']]);
lesson('mtu','IPv4 journey','MTU and fragmentation','Switching 33–35',
 'MTU is the largest IP datagram the outgoing link can carry in one frame. If the packet exceeds it, the IPv4 DF flag determines whether the router can fragment it.',
 ['Read outgoing interface MTU','Fits → send whole','Too large + DF 0 → fragment','Too large + DF 1 → discard and report MTU','Destination reassembles fragments'],
 'IPv4 length 1500, header 20, MTU 1280. First payload floor((1280−20)/8)×8 = 1256. Second payload 224. Total lengths 1276 and 244. Offsets 0 and 157.',
 [['Why divide the offset by eight?','Fragment Offset is expressed in units of eight payload bytes. Every nonfinal payload must be divisible by eight.'],['What is shared across fragments?','Identification, source, destination, and protocol identify the original datagram. MF is 1 except for the final fragment.'],['Does the next router reassemble?','Ordinary forwarding routers forward the fragments. The destination reconstructs the original datagram.']]);
lesson('egress','IPv4 journey','Output queue and transmission','Switching 13–27, 34',
 'The router transfers the datagram to the selected outgoing interface. The interface queues it if busy and supplies the frame and physical transmission for the next link.',
 ['Select output interface','Wait if its queue is busy','Build Ethernet header and FCS','Serialize bits at link rate','Signals propagate toward next hop'],
 'R1 uses its eth1 MAC as source and R2 ingress MAC as destination. IP destination remains Host B.',
 [['Can things happen concurrently?','Independent interfaces and packets can run concurrently. For one frame, the first bits can propagate while later bits are still being serialized.'],['What can cause a drop at egress?','A full queue may drop an arriving packet. Other drops can follow policy or link failures.']]);
lesson('destination','IPv4 journey','Direct delivery and reassembly','Switching 35; Delivery 3–10',
 'The final router recognizes the connected destination subnet and resolves the host on that LAN. The host checks that the datagram is for it, reassembles any fragments, and hands the payload upward.',
 ['Final router resolves destination MAC','Host accepts Ethernet frame','IP checks local destination','Reassemble if fragmented','Deliver by IP Protocol field'],
 'R2’s last-hop ARP target is 203.0.113.20, Host B itself.',
 [['How many direct deliveries occur?','In the slides’ IP path model one final direct delivery reaches the host, after zero or more indirect deliveries.'],['What if a fragment never arrives?','The incomplete reassembly eventually expires.']]);
lesson('switching','Switching','Circuit, datagram and virtual circuit','Switching 2–18, 28–30, 37–39',
 'Circuit switching reserves a path for a bit stream. Datagram switching forwards packets independently. Virtual circuit switching establishes logical forwarding state and then uses local VC identifiers.',
 ['Circuit: setup → reserved stream → teardown','Datagram: read destination → look up port per packet','Virtual circuit: setup → map incoming port and VC ID to outgoing pair → release'],
 'In a VC path, a packet can enter with VC 7 and leave with VC 12. A datagram is forwarded using its destination IP on each hop.',
 [['Does a virtual circuit always reserve bandwidth?','The virtual circuit establishes forwarding state. Resource reservation depends on the network design.'],['Why might datagrams arrive out of order?','They can queue for different times or follow different routes.']]);
lesson('delay','Switching','Transmission, propagation, queueing and processing','Switching 5–6, 13–14, 19–27',
 'Transmission is the time to put all bits onto one link. Propagation is the time for one bit to cross that link. Queueing and processing occur at devices when applicable.',
 ['Count links and switches','Compute L/R per transmitting link','Compute distance/speed per link','Add queue and processing delays when given','For circuit switching include setup as specified'],
 '5000 bits at 1 Gbps takes 5 µs. With 10 µs propagation per link, one store-and-forward switch creates two links, for 2(5+10) = 30 µs. Three switches create four links, for 60 µs. The slide’s 48,000 km example has 200 ms propagation and 0.12 ms transmission.',
 [['Why is propagation counted twice with one switch?','The bit crosses two physical links. Both propagation times contribute.'],['How do the PPT’s circuit and datagram examples differ?','Circuit delay includes setup plus the serialized stream and end-to-end propagation. Store-and-forward adds a full serialization on every link for one packet.']]);
lesson('scope','IPv4 journey','Unicast, multicast and broadcast','Routing 1, 58; ARP deck',
 'Unicast addresses one endpoint. Multicast addresses an interested group. Broadcast reaches every device on the relevant broadcast domain, with scope depending on the address and layer.',
 ['Select destination scope','Choose matching IP and link addressing','Transmit onto the network','Receivers accept based on address or group'],
 'RIPv1 uses broadcast for regular updates. RIPv2 can multicast to 224.0.0.9. An ARP request broadcasts the Ethernet frame.',
 [['Is multicasting a kind of encapsulation?','Multicast chooses recipients. Encapsulation determines which headers wrap the message. A multicast RIP message is still carried in UDP inside IP.']]);
lesson('routing','Routing','Metrics, static routes and autonomous systems','Routing 2–9',
 'Static routes are installed administratively. Dynamic routing protocols learn reachability. An autonomous system is a set of networks under a common routing administration.',
 ['Discover or configure reachability','Compare metric or policy','Install selected routes','Forward from the resulting table'],
 'RIP uses hop count. OSPF uses link cost. BGP selects routes using attributes and policy. RIP and OSPF operate within an AS, and eBGP exchanges routes between ASes.',
 [['What are control and data planes?','The control plane builds or updates forwarding information. The data plane applies it to traffic.']]);
lesson('bellman','Routing','Distance vector and Bellman–Ford','Routing 10–26',
 'A router adds its cost to each neighboring router to the distance that neighbor advertises for a destination. It selects the lowest usable candidate.',
 ['Initialize self to zero and unknown routes to infinity','Receive neighbors’ vectors','Add cost to each neighbor','Select minimum and record next hop','Repeat as updates arrive'],
 'Via R2: 1 + 2 = 3. Via R3: 1 + 4 = 5. Install cost 3 via R2. If R2 is the current next hop and raises its metric, recalculate even though that candidate got worse.',
 [['Does each router know the whole topology?','A distance-vector router exchanges estimated distances and next hops with neighbors. A link-state router builds a topology database.']]);
lesson('rip','Routing','RIP packet formats, requests and responses','Routing 38–46, 54–58',
 'RIP exchanges distance-vector updates over UDP port 520. The command distinguishes request from response. A response may answer a request or be periodic or triggered.',
 ['Send request or trigger regular update','Fill command and version','Encode destination and metric','v2 adds mask, route tag and next-hop field','Encapsulate in UDP → IPv4'],
 'Metric 1–15 is reachable. 16 means unreachable. RIPv1 omits subnet masks. RIPv2 can carry classless prefixes, authenticate exchanges and multicast.',
 [['What does address family 2 mean?','The route entry is for IPv4 in the TCP/IP family. The value is unrelated to the transport protocol.'],['Where does v2 authentication go?','When used, authentication takes the first route-entry position and indicates itself with a special address-family value.']]);
lesson('loop','Routing','Count-to-infinity and loop prevention','Routing 27–35, 45–46',
 'After a failure, a stale advertisement can make two routers think the other has a viable route. The metric rises until RIP reaches 16.',
 ['Link fails','Stale neighbor offers old route','Routers increase metric repeatedly','Split horizon omits route toward learned neighbor','Poison reverse advertises metric 16 toward that neighbor'],
 'A learned X through B. In split horizon A leaves X out of updates to B. In poison reverse A explicitly tells B that X is unreachable through A.',
 [['Are route poisoning and poison reverse the same?','Route poisoning announces a failed route with metric 16. Poison reverse is the directional advertisement back toward the neighbor used for that route.'],['Does poison reverse prevent every loop?','It addresses two-router feedback. Three-router loops can still occur.']]);
lesson('timers','Routing','RIP periodic, expiration and garbage timers','Routing 47–53',
 'The periodic timer belongs to the router. Route expiration and garbage collection apply individually to route entries.',
 ['Last update resets route expiration timer','180 seconds of silence → metric 16','Start garbage collection timer','Advertise unreachable while retained','Delete after 120 more seconds in the slide example'],
 '20 entries, five silent for 200 seconds. 1 periodic timer + 15 expiration timers + 5 garbage timers = 21. Each garbage timer has 120 − (200 − 180) = 100 seconds left.',
 [['Why is the periodic timer one?','The question describes one routing table on one router. The router schedules one regular update timer.'],['What if 20 routers were given instead?','Count the periodic timers on surviving routers. Route-specific timers require each router’s route-entry state.']]);
lesson('dijkstra','Routing','Link-state flooding and Dijkstra','Routing 59–74',
 'Each router advertises its own links. Flooding distributes the information. Each router builds a shortest-path tree rooted at itself and derives forwarding entries.',
 ['Create or refresh link-state information','Flood newer advertisements','Build the link-state database','Set root distance to zero','Settle closest unvisited node and relax neighbors','Read each destination’s first hop'],
 'R1→R2 costs 2, R1→R3 costs 4, R2→B costs 3, and R3→B costs 4. R1 reaches B via R2 at cost 5.',
 [['Do all routers get the same shortest-path tree?','Within an area they can have the same topology database, but each tree has a different root.'],['When are updates flooded?','Topology changes trigger updates. Periodic refresh prevents valid information from aging out.']]);
lesson('areas','OSPF','Areas, ABRs, ASBRs and link types','Routing 75–88',
 'Area 0 is the backbone. An ABR connects OSPF areas. An ASBR imports external routes. OSPF cost is accumulated along an internal path.',
 ['Point-to-point → two directly connected routers','Transit LAN → several routers share a network','Stub network → only one attached router','Virtual link → logical backbone connection through a transit area'],
 'If only R1 attaches to N1, N1 is a stub. If R1, R2 and R3 share N2 and form adjacencies there, N2 is a transit network.',
 [['What makes a network transit?','Traffic can enter through one participating router and exit through another. The LAN itself does not run routing.'],['What does the designated router do?','On a multiaccess segment the DR represents the shared network and originates its Type 2 Network LSA. Election uses interface priority and router ID as a tiebreaker.']]);
lesson('ospf','OSPF','OSPF packets and database synchronization','Routing 90–91, 106–109',
 'OSPFv2 uses a 24-byte common header and IP protocol 89. Packet type identifies the message carrying the information. LSA type identifies the advertisement inside.',
 ['Hello discovers and maintains neighbors','DBD lists LSA headers','LSR requests particular LSA identities','LSU carries complete LSAs','LSAck confirms receipt with LSA headers'],
 'If R1 sees an older local copy in the DBD comparison, it requests the LSA. R2 sends an LSU containing the full advertisement.',
 [['Which three fields identify a requested LSA?','LS type, Link State ID and Advertising Router.'],['What is in the common header?','Version, type, length, router ID, area ID, checksum, authentication type and authentication.'],['Does OSPF use TCP?','It runs directly over IP and handles reliable flooding through its own acknowledgment and retransmission mechanisms.']]);
lesson('lsa','OSPF','Five LSAs and who originates them','Routing 91–105',
 'An LSU can carry several LSAs. Each LSA has its own 20-byte header and body. A Router LSA can hold multiple link records.',
 ['Type 1: every router describes its area links','Type 2: DR describes the transit LAN','Type 3: ABR advertises another area’s network','Type 4: ABR advertises reachability to an ASBR','Type 5: ASBR advertises an external destination'],
 'R1, R2 and R3 share a LAN with R2 as DR. Draw all three routers connected to the LAN. Draw three Type 1 advertisements and one Type 2 from R2.',
 [['One Router LSA with three links plus one Network LSA with three router IDs. How many LSAs?','Two. Count the advertisement headers, not the link or router records within them.'],['What goes in Router and Network LSA bodies?','Router links carry link ID, link data, link type and metric, with optional TOS fields in the slide format. Network LSAs carry mask and attached router IDs.'],['Does a single-router stub LAN get a Type 2?','In OSPFv2 it is represented as a stub link in the attached router’s Type 1 LSA. The teaching slide’s single-router Type 2 example is misleading.']]);
lesson('bgp','Routing','Path vector, BGP messages and policy','Routing 110–134',
 'BGP peers exchange CIDR prefixes and path attributes in a TCP session on port 179. Import and export policy govern what is accepted, preferred or announced.',
 ['Establish TCP session','OPEN negotiates parameters','KEEPALIVE confirms and maintains session','UPDATE advertises or withdraws prefixes','NOTIFICATION reports errors and closes session'],
 'An UPDATE for 203.0.113.0/24 might carry AS_PATH 64502 64503. A router in an AS already on the path uses that information to reject a loop.',
 [['What are stub, multihomed and transit ASes?','A stub AS has one external connection. A multihomed nontransit AS has several. A transit AS carries traffic between other ASes.'],['What are eBGP and iBGP?','eBGP is between ASes. iBGP shares BGP information among peers in the same AS.'],['What do the attributes do?','ORIGIN describes the route origin, AS_PATH describes the AS sequence, and NEXT_HOP names the next hop. Attributes can be well-known or optional, with optional transitive and nontransitive categories.']]);

lesson('arp-cache','ARP','ARP cache states and modules','ARP deck · Cache Table and ARP components',
 'The output module resolves the next hop and queues datagrams if necessary. The input module processes received ARP messages. A cache-control module ages entries and manages retries.',
 ['FREE → available slot','Cache miss → PENDING, attempt 1','While pending → queue additional datagrams','Reply arrives → RESOLVED, send queued datagrams','Timeout or exhausted retries → reclaim entry'],
 'In the slide example, 114.5.7.89 is RESOLVED and transmits immediately. For 116.1.7.22, a cache miss creates a PENDING entry and starts a queue. A successful reply fills in the MAC and releases the matching queue.',
 [['What does an entry store?','State, timeout, attempts, the IP and hardware addresses when available, and a reference to the packet queue.'],['What happens if ARP attempts run out?','The slide model frees the pending entry and clears the waiting queue. The error reported to the sender depends on the device and forwarding context.'],['Is the slide’s resolved timeout of 900 seconds fixed by ARP?','No. It is an example timer value. Cache lifetime and retry limits depend on the implementation.']]);
lesson('arp-legacy','ARP','Proxy ARP, RARP and four routing cases','ARP deck · Figures 8.5 and 8.7',
 'The ARP target is always an IPv4 address on the sender’s outgoing link in these examples. Proxy ARP makes a router answer for another IP with its own MAC. Historical RARP used a known hardware address to obtain an IPv4 address.',
 ['Host to local host → resolve host IP','Host to gateway → resolve gateway IP','Router to router → resolve next router IP','Final router to host → resolve destination IP'],
 'A proxy answers who-has 141.23.56.21 with the proxy’s own MAC. The sender uses that MAC in its frame, and the proxy forwards the IP packet.',
 [['What replaced RARP?','BOOTP and later DHCP provide host configuration, including an IPv4 address.'],['Does proxy ARP change the IP destination?','The destination remains the original target IP. The frame’s destination is the proxy MAC.']]);
const upperStudy=[
 ['tcp','Transport','TCP connection and byte stream','TCP slides 2–28, 38–58, 63–72',
 'TCP offers a reliable, ordered, full-duplex byte stream between two endpoints. Sequence numbering is per byte and independent in each direction.',
 ['Open with SYN → SYN+ACK → ACK','Buffer bytes and form segments','Acknowledge the next expected byte','Retransmit missing data','Close each direction with FIN or abort with RST'],
 'With client ISN 1000, SYN uses 1000. A 1000-byte segment begins at 1001 and next ACK is 2001. A pure ACK takes no sequence space.',
 [['How many sequence numbers do SYN and FIN consume?','One each, plus any data bytes the segment carries.'],['How does half-close work?','One endpoint sends FIN and stops sending data. It can still receive bytes and acknowledge the other direction.'],['Does TCP preserve write boundaries?','It delivers a stream. The application decides how to frame messages.']],
 [['Sequence number','Number of the first data byte in the segment, or a control sequence number for SYN or FIN.','Read the Sequence field in the handshake lab.'],['Acknowledgment number','Next byte expected in the opposite direction.','ACK 3001 cumulatively confirms receipt through byte 3000.'],['ISN','Initial sequence number selected by one TCP endpoint.','Client 1000 and server 7000 are independent values.'],['SYN','TCP flag used to synchronize sequence numbers.','One SYN consumes one sequence number.'],['FIN','TCP flag closing one sending direction.','The peer can continue sending after a FIN.'],['RST','TCP flag used to reject or abort a connection.','An attempted connection to a closed port can receive a reset.'],['Half-close','Closing one direction of a full-duplex transport connection.','Try the Close and half-close sequence.'],['Full duplex','Communication in both directions at the same time.','Each TCP direction has a separately numbered byte stream.']]],
 ['tcp-fields','Transport','TCP header, windows and buffering','TCP slides 7–15, 28–37, 59–62',
 'The minimum TCP header is 20 bytes. Data offset gives header length in 32-bit words. The advertised receive window protects the receiver, and the congestion window limits flight size according to network conditions.',
 ['Read ports and sequence fields','Use data offset to locate payload','Read flags and advertised receive window','Validate checksum with IP pseudoheader','Deliver bytes in order from receive buffer'],
 'Data offset 8 means 32 header bytes and 12 bytes of options and padding. With rwnd 4000 and cwnd 3000, at most 3000 bytes may be in flight before accounting for bytes already outstanding.',
 [['Which fields follow ports and sequence numbers?','Data offset, reserved bits, flags, receive window, checksum, urgent pointer and optional fields.'],['How are flow and congestion control different?','The receive window represents receiver capacity. The congestion window responds to network conditions.'],['What are PSH and URG?','PSH is a push indication for prompt delivery. URG says the urgent pointer is meaningful. They serve different purposes.']],
 [['Data offset','TCP header size in 32-bit words.','Offset 5 denotes the 20-byte minimum.'],['Flow control','Limiting sent data according to receiver capacity.','The receiver advertises available window space.'],['Congestion control','Limiting network load according to congestion signals.','The congestion window may be smaller than the receive window.'],['Receive window (rwnd)','Advertised space available to receive data.','A receiver with limited buffering can advertise a small window.'],['Congestion window (cwnd)','Sender-side limit based on network congestion.','Usable sending is constrained by both cwnd and rwnd.'],['TCP checksum','Error detection over TCP header, data and an IP pseudoheader.','A recipient discards a corrupted TCP segment.'],['PSH','TCP push flag associated with prompt delivery of buffered data.','It does not create application message boundaries.'],['URG','TCP flag that makes the urgent pointer meaningful.','Urgent data is part of the TCP sequence space.']]],
 ['udp','Transport','UDP ports, header and checksum','UDP slides 2–45',
 'UDP is a connectionless transport for independent datagrams. It multiplexes applications using ports and offers checksum-based error detection without built-in retransmission or ordering.',
 ['Select source and destination ports','Add the 8-byte UDP header','Compute checksum using the IP pseudoheader','Pass datagram to IP','Validate and demultiplex using destination socket'],
 'CB84 000D 001C … gives source 52100, destination 13, total 28 bytes, payload 20 bytes and Daytime as the destination service. The fourth word is the checksum.',
 [['Why are IP and port both needed?','IP reaches the host interface. The transport port helps select the process endpoint.'],['How is an odd-length checksum input handled?','A zero byte pads the calculation only. The pad is not added to the UDP length.'],['What happens when UDP receives corrupted data?','A failing checksum causes discard. The application may implement a timeout and retry.']],
 [['UDP datagram','UDP header plus a single application payload.','The request and reply are independently delivered.'],['Port number','16-bit transport identifier for an endpoint.','DNS commonly receives queries on port 53.'],['Ephemeral port','A temporary local transport port typically chosen for a client.','The response returns to the original source port.'],['Port ranges','System 0–1023, registered 1024–49151, and dynamic/private 49152–65535.','An operating system may choose its own ephemeral allocation range.'],['Socket address','An IP address and port identifying a transport endpoint.','203.0.113.20:53 identifies an example DNS endpoint in a given transport.'],['Pseudoheader','IP-derived fields included only in transport checksum computation.','It is not transmitted as an extra UDP header.'],['UDP length','Combined UDP header and payload length in bytes.','A length of 28 leaves 20 data bytes.'],['Multiplexing','Combining traffic from several application endpoints into transport delivery.','Ports distinguish the sources of concurrent messages.'],['Demultiplexing','Delivering incoming traffic to the correct endpoint.','The receiving transport uses socket information to select the process.']]],
 ['application','Application','Client/server design and socket interfaces','Application slides 2–9',
 'A client initiates a service request and a server makes a service available. The socket interface lets an application use transport services through the operating system.',
 ['Client obtains an endpoint','Server binds a known endpoint','Choose iterative or concurrent service','Send and receive through the socket interface'],
 'A TCP server can accept connections from two clients on one listening port. Each accepted connection has its own pair of endpoints.',
 [['Is concurrent service the same as TCP?','Concurrency is a server design choice. The deck pairs common iterative connectionless and concurrent connection-oriented examples.'],['Can a host run two clients at once?','Yes. Their transport endpoints distinguish the exchanges.']],
 [['Client','Process that initiates a request for service.','A browser requests a representation.'],['Server','Process that provides a service to clients.','An HTTP origin answers requests.'],['Iterative server','A server that handles one request at a time.','Later requests can wait while it services the first.'],['Concurrent server','A server that can have multiple requests or connections in progress.','A slow client need not block service to another.'],['Socket interface','An operating-system API for accessing network communication.','Typical operations include bind, connect, listen, accept, send and receive.']]],
 ['dhcp','Application','DHCP allocation and leases','DHCP slides 2–34',
 'DHCP configures the client with an address, mask, gateway and DNS resolver. A static reservation maps a client to a configured address. A dynamic lease assigns an address for a finite period.',
 ['INIT sends Discover','SELECTING receives offers','REQUESTING chooses an offer','BOUND after ACK','RENEWING at T1 → REBINDING at T2 if unanswered → INIT on expiry'],
 'An eight-hour lease normally renews after four hours and rebinds after seven. A relay fills giaddr so a remote server knows the client subnet.',
 [['Why start at 0.0.0.0?','The client has no configured IPv4 address yet. It can transmit a limited broadcast locally.'],['Why use a relay?','A limited broadcast does not cross a router. The relay forwards a request to a remote server and returns replies to the client subnet.'],['How do RARP, BOOTP and DHCP relate?','RARP supplied an address from a hardware identity. BOOTP added boot configuration and relay support. DHCP extends BOOTP with lease-based allocation and more options.']],
 [['DHCPDISCOVER','Client message looking for DHCP servers.','An unconfigured client broadcasts from UDP 68 to 67.'],['DHCPOFFER','Server proposal of an address and configuration.','The proposed address appears in yiaddr.'],['DHCPREQUEST','Client message selecting, renewing, rebinding or validating an address.','Initial selection identifies the chosen server and is broadcast.'],['DHCPACK','Server confirmation of configuration or lease.','The client enters BOUND after accepting the selected configuration.'],['DHCPNAK','Server rejection of an invalid or inappropriate requested configuration.','The client can return to initialization.'],['DHCPDECLINE','Client report that an offered address is already in use.','The server can mark that address unavailable.'],['DHCPRELEASE','Client notice returning a lease early.','The server can reclaim the address.'],['DHCPINFORM','Client request for options when it already has an address.','A locally configured host can request additional settings.'],['Lease and T1/T2','Lease is the permitted use interval. T1 and T2 are renewal and rebinding points.','Defaults are typically 50% and 87.5% unless supplied otherwise.'],['DHCP relay','Agent linking a client LAN to a remote DHCP server.','giaddr identifies the client subnet in the base relay scenario.'],['DHCP transaction ID (xid)','Field correlating request and reply messages.','The client looks for a response matching its exchange.'],['ciaddr, yiaddr, siaddr, giaddr','Client address, offered client address, next-server address and relay address fields.','Inspect yiaddr on Offer and giaddr in the relay scenario.'],['DHCP broadcast flag','A client indication that it needs a broadcast reply under applicable conditions.','It can matter before the IP stack is configured.'],['BOOTP and TFTP','BOOTP is a predecessor to DHCP. TFTP can retrieve a separately specified boot file.','Configuration can name a boot server and filename.']]],
 ['dns','Application','DNS names, delegation and resolution','DNS slides 2–45',
 'DNS distributes naming data across a hierarchy of authoritative servers. A client stub can request a recursive answer from a resolver, which typically follows iterative referrals and caches the result.',
 ['Stub requests a name','Resolver checks cache','Root returns TLD referral','TLD returns delegated authority','Authority answers','Resolver caches and returns result'],
 'For www.example.com. the resolver can follow root → .com → example.com, then return an A record. With a valid cache entry it can skip those remote queries.',
 [['Are domain and zone identical?','A domain is a subtree. A zone is the portion administered together, excluding separately delegated child zones.'],['Primary vs secondary?','Both can answer authoritatively. The primary has the maintained zone data. A secondary copies it through zone transfer.'],['How does reverse DNS work?','Reverse the IPv4 octets, append in-addr.arpa and query PTR. It is separate from forward naming.']],
 [['Domain name space','The hierarchy of labels used in DNS.','A root label anchors TLDs and delegated domains.'],['Flat vs hierarchical naming','Flat names have no grouping structure. Hierarchical names express relationships through labels.','DNS uses hierarchical delegation to distribute administration.'],['Label','A component of a domain name.','example and com are separate labels.'],['FQDN','A name that specifies the entire hierarchy to the DNS root.','www.example.com. explicitly includes the final root dot.'],['PQDN','A partially qualified name that can require local completion.','A resolver may append a configured search suffix to a short hostname.'],['DNS zone','An administered part of the DNS hierarchy bounded by delegation.','A delegated child can have a separate zone and authoritative service.'],['Root, TLD and authoritative servers','Servers at the root, top-level domain and delegated zone stages.','A referral leads the resolver toward the authority that can answer.'],['Primary and secondary DNS','Authoritative servers with maintained source data and an authoritative transferred copy, respectively.','Either can answer from its own authoritative copy.'],['Recursive resolution','Providing a final answer or error on behalf of the requester.','The stub asks the recursive resolver to resolve the whole name.'],['Iterative resolution','Answering directly if possible or giving a referral to a server closer to the name.','The resolver follows the root’s TLD referral.'],['DNS cache TTL','Remaining permitted lifetime for a cached DNS record.','After expiry the resolver should refresh the record when needed.'],['Registrar','A service through which domain registrations are managed.','Registration and authoritative hosting can be supplied by different organizations.'],['Generic and country-code TLDs','Top-level namespaces such as .com and .in.','The first referral for example.com points toward .com.']]],
 ['dns-message','Application','DNS message format and resource records','DNS slides 46 onward',
 'A DNS message has a 12-byte header followed by question, answer, authority and additional sections. Resource records carry typed data. Compression can reuse a previously encoded name.',
 ['Match query to response with ID','Read QR, opcode and other flags','Decode question and section counts','Parse resource records and compression pointers','Retry with TCP when appropriate'],
 'DNS query flags 0x0100 set RD. A typical recursive response 0x8180 sets QR, RD and RA and has RCODE zero. A name pointer such as 0xC00C points to offset 12.',
 [['What do A, AAAA and PTR carry?','A gives IPv4, AAAA gives IPv6, and PTR typically carries a name for reverse DNS.'],['What if a UDP response is truncated?','TC indicates truncation. The client can retry using TCP.'],['What are AA, RD, RA and RCODE?','Authoritative Answer, Recursion Desired, Recursion Available and response status code.']],
 [['DNS header ID','Identifier used to associate a DNS response with its query.','The response includes the ID of the matching query.'],['DNS flag fields','QR, Opcode, AA, TC, RD, RA and RCODE describe how the message was sent and handled.','QR distinguishes query from response. TC can signal TCP retry.'],['DNS question record','QNAME, QTYPE and QCLASS specifying what the client asks.','www.example.com. IN A asks for an IPv4 address.'],['Resource record (RR)','Typed DNS data with name, type, class, TTL, RDLENGTH and RDATA.','An A record contains four address bytes.'],['A, AAAA, NS, CNAME, SOA, PTR and MX','Record types for IPv4, IPv6, name server, alias, zone authority, pointer and mail exchange.','MX supplies mail exchanger information and preference.'],['DNS compression pointer','A two-bit 11 prefix and 14-bit offset referencing a name in the same message.','C00C references byte offset 12.'],['Reverse DNS and PTR','Resolving an address to a name using a reverse namespace and pointer record.','203.0.113.20 maps to the lookup name 20.113.0.203.in-addr.arpa.'],['NXDOMAIN','DNS response code indicating the queried domain name does not exist.','It differs from an existing name that simply lacks the requested record type.']]],
 ['http','Application','HTTP messages, documents and connections','HTTP deck · messages, documents, persistence',
 'HTTP exchanges requests and responses carrying representations. Classic HTTP/1.1 messages use a start line, headers, blank line and an optional body.',
 ['Client identifies an origin and target','Open TCP or reuse an existing connection','Send request line and headers','Read response status and headers','Consume body and request embedded objects'],
 'GET /index.html HTTP/1.1 followed by Host: example.com can yield HTTP/1.1 200 OK and text/html. A referenced image usually requires its own request.',
 [['What do 1xx through 5xx mean?','Informational, successful, redirection, client error and server error.'],['GET, HEAD and POST?','GET asks for a representation, HEAD requests corresponding headers without a response body, and POST submits data for processing.'],['How do persistent and nonpersistent connections differ?','Persistent connections can carry multiple exchanges. A classic nonpersistent connection closes after one exchange.']],
 [['HTTP','Application protocol for exchanging requests and responses for web resources.','The browser sends GET and the origin answers.'],['URL','Identifier giving the scheme, authority and applicable path or other components.','https://example.com/catalog identifies a resource at that origin.'],['Request line','HTTP/1.1 start line with method, target and version.','GET /index.html HTTP/1.1.'],['Status line','HTTP/1.1 start line with version, status code and optional reason phrase.','HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found.'],['HTTP status code','Number describing how the request was handled.','200 is success, 304 is cache validation, and 404 means the resource was not found.'],['HTTP methods','Named request semantics such as GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, DELETE and OPTIONS.','GET retrieves a representation and PUT requests replacement of the target state.'],['Static, dynamic and active documents','Stored content, server-generated content and client-executed code in the slide classification.','A static HTML file, server-generated search results and a browser script illustrate the three.'],['Persistent connection','Connection that stays open for further request-response exchanges.','HTTP/1.1 defaults to persistence when the connection is reusable.'],['Nonpersistent connection','Connection closed after its request-response exchange.','A sequential fetch opens another TCP connection for the image.']]],
 ['http-state','Application','HTTP cookies, caching and conditional requests','HTTP deck · cookies and conditional requests',
 'Cookies let a browser return server-provided state. HTTP caching can reuse a stored response when fresh and use a validator before reusing a stale response.',
 ['Server sends Set-Cookie or cache validator','Browser stores applicable state','Later request sends Cookie or a conditional header','Server handles state or checks representation validator','304 reuses the cached representation; 200 can return new content'],
 'Request If-None-Match: "v1" yields 304 without a body if the selected representation still matches. A changed representation can yield 200 with new content.',
 [['What is the difference between cookie and cache?','A cookie carries client-associated state back to a server. A cache stores reusable representations and metadata.'],['How does If-Modified-Since work?','A client submits a known modification time. The server can answer 304 when the representation has not changed since then.']],
 [['Cookie and Set-Cookie','Set-Cookie supplies a cookie in a response. Cookie returns applicable stored values in a request.','A session identifier can be returned with later matching requests.'],['Conditional request','A request whose outcome depends on a validator or precondition.','If-None-Match checks an entity tag before sending the resource again.'],['ETag and Last-Modified','Validators based on an entity tag or a modification timestamp.','The client can use them for later cache revalidation.'],['304 Not Modified','Status indicating a conditional retrieval can use a previously stored representation.','The response has no message body.'],['Web cache and proxy','A cache stores reusable responses. A proxy is an intermediary and may include caching.','A fresh cache result can avoid a fetch from the origin.']]]
];
